Sony's game streaming service  PlayStation now is finally live allowing  you to stream retail and digital  PlayStation 3 games straight to your ps4  which normally lacks native support from  legacy console libraries PlayStation 4  is the first PlayStation now device to  graduate out of beta status in our early  tests indicate that Sony's actually off  to a great start the PlayStation now  service comes in two flavors monthly  subscriptions and individual game  rentals there are two subscription tiers  that offer unlimited access to over 100  PlayStation 3 games you can spend $20  for a single month or save 15 with a  three month commitment at $45 rental  pricing is more complicated and it  varies from game to game this is because  publishers ultimately have control over  pricing and rental periods in most cases  you can expect to pay as little as $2  for a 4 hour rental  most games offer 90-day rentals for $15  or less but there are a handful that  come in at higher price points nascar 14  cost $40 for 90 days and MotoGP 13 will  set you back 30 bucks  the worst offender however is f1 2013  which cost $50 to rent for 90 days some  games such as XCOM enemy within don't  even have a 90 day rental period oddly  XCOM is the most expensive game per day  at 30 dollars for 30 days I don't like  the looks of this because your streaming  games over the internet from Sony's  servers it's imperative that you not  only have a good internet connection but  also that your network at home is up to  the task according to Sony you need to  be able to download data at a rate of at  least 5 megabits per second anything  less you may experience choppy audio and  video rendering some games unplayable  we tested PlayStation now on a hard  wired network connection rated at 35  megabits per second and on a Wi-Fi  network rated at 10 megabits per second  what we found was that PlayStation now  works far better than expected even on  Wi-Fi I can't say that I had high hopes  of games both looking and playing well  but they did including late intensity  games like Super Street Fighter 4 Arcade  Edition which felt every bit as  responsive as it needed  granted I don't play on a competitive  level but I play enough Street Fighter  to understand the speed and rhythm of  certain characters and favorites like  Ibuki ryu and el fuerte felt the same as  they always have even on the slower  Wi-Fi connection so does that mean that  PlayStation now is a seamless experience  as as good as playing game straight from  the disc well it may be close but it's  not the same every game we played  exhibited video compression artifacts  groups of blurry pixels that result from  Sony reducing the bitrate of the raw  video so that it's fit for streaming on  most internet connections in most cases  the amount of artifacts on screen were  minimal and easy to ignore it's  prevalent during scenes with a lot of  grapes but generally speaking the  quality of sony's video stream is  sufficient and Mensa if you're worried  about online multiplayer fear not  because it works exactly as it would if  you were playing on an actual ps3  whether there are still people playing  older games online is another discussion  but on Sony's end it's done a good job  of preserving the experience of playing  with real Hardware in salt so in the  first week of Sony launching PlayStation  now it's proven to be a service that's  actually worth some of the costs  associated some games that can be had  for less than 10 bucks for 90 days for  example that's a pretty good deal of  course you also don't own this game it  is just a rental and if you're  subscribing you may have a limited  access for the amount of time but  eventually that subscription will also  go away and so will your access so it's  a question of value do you value games  as permanent objects that you can own or  do you want to pay a little bit of money  for an experience that you can have sort  of on a whim that's really where  PlayStation now fits in I suspect that  people will use this service probably  every PlayStation 4 owner at some point  it's just too convenient to rent a game  that maybe you've never played and I've  always wanted to I personally am NOT  going to be subscribing right away  but what I am going to do is check out  the rental service as it goes forward a  game like Final Fantasy 13 that I  haven't played since it came out I want  to revisit and you know what I don't  want to hook up my PlayStation 3 to my  TV because I don't have room for any  more contours so Sony has a long road  ahead of it for now it's just getting  started and it's going to come to many  more devices in probably the next few  months it's an open beta right now on  Vita and even PlayStation 3 if Sony can  fulfill their promise to bring you know  more consoles to now including  PlayStation 4 games PlayStation 1 and  PlayStation 2 games it's going to be a  service that's going to be really hard  for someone like Microsoft to meet  Nintendo on the other hand  maybe could do it they've got a huge  history of games behind them but are  they able to do that do they have the  technology in the know-how there knows  it took Sony to acquire Gaikai to make  it happen well stay tuned new games  watch comm for more PlayStation now  content as it develops
 

